{"answerText":"**No.** Because the combined fuel-burning-appliance input is 400,000 Btu/h—greater than 350,000 Btu/h—combustion and dilution air must be obtained **solely from the outdoors**. Meeting an otherwise applicable indoor-air volume calculation does not permit an all-indoor-air approach at this input level. (FGC §§ 304.1, 304.5)

FGC § 304.5 generally allows required indoor-air volume to be determined under § 304.5.1 or § 304.5.2, but its exception requires outdoor-only combustion, ventilation, and dilution air for fuel-burning appliances above 350,000 Btu/h. FGC § 304.1 independently states that combustion and dilution air for appliances above that threshold must be obtained solely from outdoors; it allows ventilation air to be provided by any suitable means. The 400,000-Btu/h combined input exceeds the threshold by 50,000 Btu/h.

Outdoor combustion air must be provided through openings to outdoors under FGC § 304.6. The supplied text requires each air opening to have a minimum dimension of 3 inches and requires compliance with any applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules for sizing the openings. The exact outdoor-air method, opening area, and configuration must be selected and sized under the applicable provisions not fully supplied here.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Outdoor-only combustion and dilution air above 350,000 Btu/h","explanation":"For fuel-burning appliances with input greater than 350,000 Btu/h, combustion and dilution air must be obtained solely from outdoors. FGC 304.1 states this directly; FGC 304.5 contains the same threshold in its exception, including ventilation air. Thus, an indoor-volume calculation cannot authorize all-indoor combustion air for a 400,000-Btu/h combined appliance input.","sectionID":"8078","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8078-2","research-permitext_discovered-id:8088-1"]},{"heading":"Indoor-air calculation is otherwise a prescribed method","explanation":"FGC 304.5 ordinarily bases required indoor volume on Section 304.5.1 or 304.5.2, requires use of Section 304.5.2 where known infiltration is below 0.40 ACH, and requires the total volume to reflect all appliances in the space. Its exception controls for fuel-burning appliances above 350,000 Btu/h.","sectionID":"8088","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8088-1"]},{"heading":"Outdoor-air opening baseline","explanation":"Where outdoor combustion air is required, it must be provided through openings to outdoors under FGC 304.6. That section requires a minimum air-opening dimension of 3 inches and also requires sizing to comply with applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules.","sectionID":"8094","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_cross_reference-id:8094-9"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The proposed outdoor-combustion-air method and the resulting required opening area, number, location, and configuration under FGC Section 304.6.1 or 304.6.2 are not established by the supplied evidence.","Any applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules affecting the sizing of openings connecting the room to the outdoor-air supply are not supplied.","Whether appliance manufacturer instructions impose combustion- or dilution-air requirements that apply to the identified appliances is not established."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["Permitext limited this answer to the enacted evidence assembled for the current question. The supplied evidence establishes the outdoor-only threshold and a minimum opening dimension, but does not include the text of FGC Sections 304.6.1 and 304.6.2 needed to determine the final outdoor-air opening design."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["The selected FGC Section 304.6.1 or 304.6.2 outdoor-combustion-air method and the project dimensions needed to size and locate its opening(s).","The applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules, if any, governing outdoor-air opening sizing for this installation.","Manufacturer installation instructions for each appliance, particularly if an appliance is direct-vent, non-natural-draft, non-Category-I, or equipped with a power burner."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"8078","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8078-2"],"relevance":"FGC 304.1 directly requires combustion and dilution air solely from outdoors where fuel-burning appliance input exceeds 350,000 Btu/h and distinguishes ventilation air."},{"sectionID":"8088","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8088-1"],"relevance":"FGC 304.5 establishes the indoor-air calculation framework and its exception requiring combustion, ventilation, and dilution air solely from outdoors above 350,000 Btu/h."},{"sectionID":"8094","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_cross_reference-id:8094-9"],"relevance":"FGC 304.6 establishes that outdoor combustion air is supplied through outdoor openings and sets a 3-inch minimum opening dimension."}]}

{"answerText":"**Not necessarily.** The stated **combined** 400,000 Btu/h input does not by itself establish that indoor-only combustion air is prohibited, because the supplied provisions apply the outdoor-only threshold to a *fuel-burning appliance with an input greater than 350,000 Btu/h*. If any individual appliance exceeds 350,000 Btu/h, combustion and dilution air must come solely from outdoors; under FGC § 304.5, ventilation air must also come solely from outdoors. If no individual appliance exceeds that threshold, the supplied provisions do not establish an outdoor-only requirement merely from the 400,000-Btu/h combined total. (FGC §§ 304.1, 304.5)

For an installation eligible to use indoor air, FGC § 304.5 requires the indoor-air volume to be determined under § 304.5.1 or § 304.5.2, with the required volume summed for all appliances in the space. The known-infiltration-rate method is mandatory where the known infiltration rate is below 0.40 ACH. Thus, the stated adequate indoor-air volume can support an indoor-air approach only if no individual appliance triggers the greater-than-350,000-Btu/h outdoor-only rule and the applicable § 304.5 method is satisfied. (FGC § 304.5)

The material missing fact is each appliance’s individual input rating. If outdoor air is required, the supplied text directs use of an outdoor-air method under FGC § 304.6.1 or § 304.6.2; FGC § 304.6 also sets a 3-inch minimum opening dimension and refers to any applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection sizing rules. Those detailed outdoor-air methods and any applicable Department of Environmental Protection rules were not supplied.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"The outdoor-only threshold applies per appliance","explanation":"FGC 304.1 requires combustion and dilution air to be obtained solely from outdoors for a fuel-burning appliance with an input greater than 350,000 Btu/h, while FGC 304.5 states that combustion, ventilation, and dilution air shall be obtained solely from outdoors for a fuel-burning appliance above that input. The supplied text does not state that the inputs of multiple appliances are aggregated for this threshold.","sectionID":"8078","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8078-2","research-permitext_discovered-id:8088-1"]},{"heading":"Indoor-air volume is calculated for all appliances in the space","explanation":"For indoor combustion air, FGC 304.5 requires use of Section 304.5.1 or 304.5.2, requires Section 304.5.2 where the known air-infiltration rate is less than 0.40 ACH, and requires the total volume to be the sum of the volume calculated for all appliances in the space. Its outdoor-only exception remains applicable to an individual fuel-burning appliance with input greater than 350,000 Btu/h.","sectionID":"8088","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8088-1"]},{"heading":"Outdoor-air opening baseline when outdoor air is required","explanation":"FGC 304.6 requires outdoor combustion air to be provided through opening(s) to outdoors under Section 304.6.1 or 304.6.2. Air openings must have a minimum dimension of 3 inches, and their size must also comply with any applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules.","sectionID":"8094","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_cross_reference-id:8094-9"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The individual input rating of each fuel-burning appliance; this determines whether any appliance exceeds the greater-than-350,000-Btu/h outdoor-only threshold.","If outdoor air is required, the selected FGC Section 304.6.1 or 304.6.2 method and the project-specific opening area, number, location, and configuration needed to apply it.","Any applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules affecting the size of openings connecting the room to the outdoor-air supply.","Whether manufacturer instructions impose combustion- or dilution-air requirements for any identified appliance, particularly if it is direct-vent, other than natural draft, vented but not Category I, or equipped with a power burner."] ,"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["Permitext limited this answer to the enacted evidence assembled for the current question. The supplied evidence does not include FGC §§ 304.6.1 or 304.6.2, so it does not establish the final outdoor-air opening design if outdoor air is required."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["Each appliance’s rated input, to determine whether an individual appliance exceeds 350,000 Btu/h.","The text of the selected FGC § 304.6.1 or § 304.6.2 outdoor-air method and the project dimensions needed to size and locate its opening(s), if outdoor air is required.","Any applicable New York City Department of Environmental Protection rules governing outdoor-air opening sizing.","Manufacturer installation instructions for each appliance."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"8078","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8078-2"],"relevance":"FGC 304.1 provides the per-appliance greater-than-350,000-Btu/h outdoor-only requirement for combustion and dilution air and identifies manufacturer-instruction cases."},{"sectionID":"8088","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:8088-1"],"relevance":"FGC 304.5 establishes the indoor-air calculation framework, the aggregation of calculated indoor volume for all appliances, and the per-appliance outdoor-only exception including ventilation air."},{"sectionID":"8094","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_cross_reference-id:8094-9"],"relevance":"FGC 304.6 provides the supplied baseline for outdoor combustion-air openings if the outdoor-only condition applies."}]}

{"answerText":"**No.** A 20-dwelling-unit building is above the “more than three dwelling units” threshold. The application must state: (1) the total number of units at filing, (2) the number occupied at filing, and (3) the number that will be occupied during the work. Omitting the latter two counts does not comply with AC § 28-105.5.2.

The actual occupied-unit count at filing and the count proposed to remain occupied during the work are presently unresolved and must be supplied in the application. If a unit not initially counted as occupied will later be occupied during the work, the application must be amended before that occupancy occurs (AC § 28-105.5.2).

If any dwelling unit will be occupied during construction, a tenant protection plan is required. It must identify, in sufficient detail, the specific units that are or may be occupied during construction, and the required plan must be approved before a permit may issue (AC § 28-120.1).","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Required occupied-building application counts","explanation":"For permit work on a building having more than three dwelling units, the application must state the total number of units at filing, the number occupied at filing, and the number to be occupied during the work. The established 20-unit count exceeds that threshold.","sectionID":"9003","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:9003-2"]},{"heading":"Amendment before later occupancy","explanation":"The work-permit application must be amended before a unit not initially counted as occupied during the work becomes occupied.","sectionID":"9003","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:9003-2"]},{"heading":"Tenant protection plan if units will remain occupied","explanation":"For alteration, construction, or partial demolition where any dwelling unit will be occupied during construction, a tenant protection plan must be prepared and filed. It must identify in sufficient detail the units that are or may be occupied, and no permit may issue where the plan is required unless it is approved.","sectionID":"9248","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:9248-10"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The number of dwelling units occupied when the application is filed.","The number of dwelling units that will be occupied during the work."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["This answer is limited to the supplied enacted 2022 NYC Administrative Code provisions. It does not determine other application, construction-document, or tenant-protection-plan content requirements outside the supplied evidence."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":[],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"9003","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:9003-2"],"relevance":"Establishes the three required unit-count disclosures for permit applications involving buildings with more than three dwelling units, plus the amendment requirement for later occupancy."},{"sectionID":"9248","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:9248-10"],"relevance":"Establishes the conditional tenant protection plan requirement and its approval and occupied-unit-identification requirements."}]}

{"answerText":"No. A listed commercial food-waste disposer cannot be used in the restaurant kitchen unless the Department of Environmental Protection has approved it for use. The Plumbing Code states: “Commercial food waste disposers shall be prohibited unless approved for use by the Department of Environmental Protection.” (PC § 413.3.) Product listing is therefore not a substitute for DEP approval.

The separate listing-and-labeling language applies to domestic disposers and does not authorize this commercial installation. Domestic disposers must conform to ASSE 1008, be listed and labeled under UL 430, and are permitted only within dwelling units; those requirements do not create an alternative approval path for a restaurant’s commercial disposer. (PC § 413.1.) If DEP approval is obtained, the disposer must also have a cold-water supply. (PC § 413.4.)

For professional reliance, obtain the actual DEP approval applicable to the proposed product/use; the supplied evidence does not establish DEP’s approval process, scope, or any conditions attached to an approval.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"DEP approval is required for commercial disposers","explanation":"Commercial food waste disposers are prohibited unless approved for use by the Department of Environmental Protection. A product listing alone does not satisfy this separate, express approval condition.","sectionID":"11971","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11971-1"]},{"heading":"Domestic listing provisions do not authorize a restaurant disposer","explanation":"The Code’s ASSE 1008 and UL 430 listing-and-labeling requirements apply to domestic food waste disposers, which are permitted only within dwelling units. Those domestic provisions do not establish permission to use a commercial disposer in a restaurant kitchen.","sectionID":"11969","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11969-3"]},{"heading":"Cold water is required","explanation":"Food waste disposers must be provided with a supply of cold water.","sectionID":"11972","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11972-9"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The actual DEP approval for the proposed commercial disposer, including its applicability to the proposed product and use, has not been provided."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["Permitext limited this answer to the enacted evidence assembled for the current question. The supplied evidence does not establish DEP’s approval procedure, approval documentation, or any project-specific conditions DEP may impose."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["The DEP approval or written determination applicable to the proposed commercial disposer and restaurant use."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"11971","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11971-1"],"relevance":"Direct governing prohibition unless DEP approves the commercial food waste disposer."},{"sectionID":"11969","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11969-3"],"relevance":"Establishes that listing and labeling requirements cited in the supplied evidence are for domestic disposers and that domestic disposers are limited to dwelling units."},{"sectionID":"11972","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11972-9"],"relevance":"Establishes the cold-water-supply requirement for food waste disposers."}]}
